{"entries":{"copy|Gender and age differences between women and their physicians can also":["u105"],"copy|present barriers to communication about sexual matters.":["u113"],"copy|Currently, an estimated 3 million older people with cognitive impairment or dementia are both community-dwelling":["u126"],"copy|and sexually active.":["u128"],"copy|Sometimes,":["u135"],"copy|sexual relationships form among people with dementia who have, but may not remember, a communityresiding spouse.":["u137"],"copy|Communication of normative expectations to":["u173"],"copy|patients about the longevity and quality of their sexually active lives assuming good health could motivate patients to stop smoking, adhere to medication regimens, exercise regularly, or engage in other health-promoting behaviors.":["u179"],"copy|Viral infections, including genital herpes simplex virus (HSV) and human papillomavirus (HPV), are the":["u198"],"copy|more prevalent STIs among older women.":["u199"],"copy|TABLE 35-3 ■ MOST COMMON SEXUAL PROBLEMS AMONG A POPULATION-BASED SAMPLE OF OLDER WOMEN IN THE UNITED STATES (N = 1550)":["u199"],"copy|HSV-2 seroprevalence among men and women 70 years and older based on NHANES III data (1988–1994) was 28%, but women had a higher overall prevalence than men and rates were much higher among African-Americans (74%) and Mexican Americans (45%).":["u200"],"copy|In acute or emergency care settings, intimate partner violence and sexual abuse should be considered when a woman presents with physical injury, vague symptoms (especially in recurrent visits), acute mental status changes,":["u245"],"copy|and/or is accompanied by a partner or other individual who interferes with the patient’s interaction with health care providers.":["u246"],"copy|TABLE 35-4 ■ SAMPLE INTIMATE PARTNER VIOLENCE SCREENING QUESTIONS":["u246"],"copy|Disruptions in these physiologic systems caused by age-related changes, physical or mental illness, or medical procedures and medication use can alter the sexual response and the ability of a":["u272"],"copy|woman to derive pleasure and satisfaction from her sexual encounters.":["u286"],"copy|While levels of endogenous androgens have not been found to be predictive of sexual function, a large meta-analysis confirmed that testosterone":["u304"],"copy|therapy for low libido was effective in clinical trials involving women with bilateral oophorectomy or total loss of ovarian function, as well as postmenopausal women with or without use of estrogen therapy (estrogen reduces bioavailable testosterone by increasing sex hormone–binding globulin production).":["u306"],"copy|Prevalence estimates for older women can be estimated from prior studies, but are needed for the newly":["u403"],"copy|defined classes of genitopelvic pain/penetration disorder and female sexual interest/arousal disorder.":["u405"],"copy|Although many adult female sexual function problems have a psychosocial component, a thorough gynecologic physical examination by a skilled practitioner is needed to":["u436"],"copy|evaluate for underlying gynecologic pathologies that may be contributing to sexual function issues.":["u438"],"copy|Pelvic physical therapy is an":["u457"],"copy|effective modality for treating dyspareunia when there is a pelvic, abdominal, and/or low-back component to the dyspareunia and when vaginismus is present.":["u458"],"copy|TABLE 35-11 ■ OVERVIEW OF VAGINAL LUBRICANTS AND MOISTURIZERS":["u458"],"copy|Vaginal dilation with graded dilators or other devices and behavioral, sex, or couples’ therapy are additional modalities that benefit many women with sexual pain disorders.":["u459"]},"maxId":0,"sequenceOffsets":[[105,1],[112,2],[124,3],[125,4],[131,5],[132,6],[167,7],[172,8],[190,11],[234,13],[259,14],[272,15],[289,16],[290,17],[386,18],[387,19],[417,20],[418,21],[436,24],[589,25]],"preservedBreaks":{"Gender and age differences between women and their physicians can also present barriers to communication about sexual matters.":[70],"Currently, an estimated 3 million older people with cognitive impairment or dementia are both community-dwelling and sexually active.":[112],"Sometimes, sexual relationships form among people with dementia who have, but may not remember, a communityresiding spouse.":[10],"Communication of normative expectations to patients about the longevity and quality of their sexually active lives assuming good health could motivate patients to stop smoking, adhere to medication regimens, exercise regularly, or engage in other health-promoting behaviors.":[42],"Viral infections, including genital herpes simplex virus (HSV) and human papillomavirus (HPV), are the more prevalent STIs among older women.":[102],"TABLE 35-3 ■ MOST COMMON SEXUAL PROBLEMS AMONG A POPULATION-BASED SAMPLE OF OLDER WOMEN IN THE UNITED STATES (N = 1550) HSV-2 seroprevalence among men and women 70 years and older based on NHANES III data (1988–1994) was 28%, but women had a higher overall prevalence than men and rates were much higher among African-Americans (74%) and Mexican Americans (45%).":[119],"In acute or emergency care settings, intimate partner violence and sexual abuse should be considered when a woman presents with physical injury, vague symptoms (especially in recurrent visits), acute mental status changes, and/or is accompanied by a partner or other individual who interferes with the patient’s interaction with health care providers.":[222],"Disruptions in these physiologic systems caused by age-related changes, physical or mental illness, or medical procedures and medication use can alter the sexual response and the ability of a woman to derive pleasure and satisfaction from her sexual encounters.":[191],"While levels of endogenous androgens have not been found to be predictive of sexual function, a large meta-analysis confirmed that testosterone therapy for low libido was effective in clinical trials involving women with bilateral oophorectomy or total loss of ovarian function, as well as postmenopausal women with or without use of estrogen therapy (estrogen reduces bioavailable testosterone by increasing sex hormone–binding globulin production).":[143],"Prevalence estimates for older women can be estimated from prior studies, but are needed for the newly defined classes of genitopelvic pain/penetration disorder and female sexual interest/arousal disorder.":[102],"Although many adult female sexual function problems have a psychosocial component, a thorough gynecologic physical examination by a skilled practitioner is needed to evaluate for underlying gynecologic pathologies that may be contributing to sexual function issues.":[165],"Pelvic physical therapy is an effective modality for treating dyspareunia when there is a pelvic, abdominal, and/or low-back component to the dyspareunia and when vaginismus is present.":[29],"TABLE 35-11 ■ OVERVIEW OF VAGINAL LUBRICANTS AND MOISTURIZERS Vaginal dilation with graded dilators or other devices and behavioral, sex, or couples’ therapy are additional modalities that benefit many women with sexual pain disorders.":[61]}}
